//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="child said: 0")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: std.process, std.net, os.proc.inherit, err.row, err.propagate
//!
//! **A PARENT AND A CHILD, IN ONE TEST, FOR THE FIRST TIME IN THIS
//! REPOSITORY.** Every earlier process and net witness here proves a
//! REFUSAL, because the rig runs one program per test and no program
//! could start a useful second one: `net/adopt_rows.lu` says in as many
//! words that it cannot reach the adoptable case, and F-0066 records why
//! the happy path of a spawn was unwitnessable at all. Both halves of
//! `[os.proc.inherit]` now exist at the std tier — `process.start_with`
//! (sc39) and `net.adopt_listener` (sc37) — and with `os_exe` (s90) a
//! program can spawn ITSELF, which is the third thing the handshake
//! needed. So this file runs the whole prefork handoff end to end.
//!
//! **The proof is the PORT.** The parent binds an ephemeral loopback
//! listener, reads its port, and spawns itself with that port as its one
//! argument and the listener as its one inherit entry. The child adopts
//! descriptor **3** — "the listener is 3" is true by position, which is
//! the clause's whole contract — reads the port off what it adopted, and
//! exits `0` only if the two numbers agree. Nothing is told to the child
//! out of band except the number it must MATCH, so an accidental pass is
//! not available: a child that adopted something else, or nothing, cannot
//! produce the parent's ephemeral port.
//!
//! **The second assertion is the BOUND, and nothing else witnesses it.**
//! `[os.proc.inherit]` says "nothing above `2 + len(inherit)` is open in
//! the child that the runtime put there". The set here has ONE entry, so
//! descriptor **4** must not be an adoptable listener — the child asks
//! for it and requires `io`. That sentence is a promise about what is
//! ABSENT, and a test is the only place a promise about absence can live.
//!
//! **Exit codes are the channel, deliberately.** The child's verdict
//! comes back as its exit code (`0` agree, `7` port mismatch, `8`
//! descriptor 4 adopted something) and the parent prints only what it was
//! handed. Two negative controls were run against this shape before it
//! landed and both behaved: handing the child a port one digit off makes
//! it exit `7`, and spawning with an EMPTY inherit set makes its
//! `adopt_listener(3)` answer `io` and ride out of `main`. The file
//! cannot hold either control — a witness gets one program — so they are
//! recorded here, which is the only place they can be.
//!
//! **Native only, and the two reasons are different.** A non-empty
//! inherit set is refused by name on the checked machine and on lupin
//! ("fd inheritance across os_spawn_with in checked execution"), so
//! neither lane reaches the spawn. Underneath that there is a SECOND
//! reason a self-spawn is native-only, worth writing down because it will
//! bite the next person who tries this shape: **under an interpreter
//! `os_exe` names the INTERPRETER, not the program.** Measured at sc39 —
//! on the checked lane the "child" is the `wolf` binary handed an
//! argument it does not know, which prints a usage line and exits 2; on
//! lupin the same, exit 2. A self-spawn witness is only meaningful where
//! the running executable IS the program, which is the native rung alone.

use std.env
use std.net
use std.process

fn main() -> !int {
    let argv = env.args()
    if argv.len > 0 {
        let want = argv[0]
        var adopted = net.adopt_listener(3)?
        let got = net.port(adopted)?
        net.close_listener(take adopted)?
        if "{got}" != want { return 7 }
        var past_the_set = false
        let over = net.adopt_listener(4) else |e| match e {
            unsupported => { net.Listener { fd: 0 - 1 } },
            io => {
                past_the_set = true
                net.Listener { fd: 0 - 1 }
            },
        }
        if !past_the_set { return 8 }
        if over.fd != 0 - 1 { return 9 }
        return 0
    }
    var srv = net.listen(net.loopback(0))?
    let port = net.port(srv)?
    var set = List[net.Listener]()
    (mut set).push(srv)
    var cmd = process.command(os_exe()?)
    process.push_arg(mut cmd, "{port}")
    var ch = process.start_with(cmd, set)?
    let code = process.wait(take ch)?
    net.close_listener(take srv)?
    print("child said: {code}")
    if code != 0 { return code }
    0
}
